  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses solely on the probability of a favorable outcome (interpreted as 0 Euros), treating all losses as negligible differences. The individual does consider the probability of 0 Euros (75% in A vs 50% in B), which aligns with the reason's relevant information. However, the individual also discusses the magnitudes of losses (-6000 vs -4000 vs -2000), indicating that they do not treat outcome differences as negligible. This contradicts a core assumption of the decision reason. While the individual acknowledges the higher chance of no loss in A—consistent with the reason’s rule—they integrate outcome magnitudes into their justification, suggesting a more nuanced or different decision rule. Therefore, although part of the reasoning overlaps (attention to no-loss probability), the individual does not fully adopt the simplifying assumption of negligible outcome differences. The justification includes elements outside the scope of the decision reason.",
